GCC runtime library (libgcc)
----------------------------

The libgcc pieces listed above come from GCC (Copyright Free Software
Foundation, Inc.), which is licensed under the GNU General Public License
with an exception that allows linking its runtime code into programs
without those programs becoming covered by the GPL. GCC 4.3.3 is older than
the "GCC Runtime Library Exception" (GCC 4.4 and later, file
COPYING.RUNTIME); in the 4.3 series the exception was written into the
header of each runtime source file.

Not found: the WinAVR install used to build the hex contains neither a
COPYING.RUNTIME file nor the source of the AVR libgcc (libgcc.S), so the
exact exception wording of the files linked here could not be copied from
it and is not reproduced here. For comparison only, the same toolchain
release has these exceptions in files that were found:

  - GCC's own headers installed with it, for example
    C:\WinAVR\lib\gcc\avr\4.3.3\include\stdarg.h, say:

    "As a special exception, if you include this header file into source
     files compiled by GCC, this header file does not by itself cause
     the resulting executable to be covered by the GNU General Public
     License.  This exception does not however invalidate any other
     reasons why the executable file might be covered by the GNU General
     Public License."

  - The GCC 4.3.2 start-up file for the AVR32 port, gcc/config/avr32/crtn.asm
    (in C:\WinAVR\source\avr32\gcc\4.3.2\30-gcc-4.3.2-avr32.patch), says:

    "In addition to the permissions in the GNU General Public License, the
     Free Software Foundation gives you unlimited permission to link the
     compiled version of this file with other programs, and to distribute
     those programs without any restriction coming from the use of this
     file.  (The General Public License restrictions do apply in other
